  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Cold mornings and pleasant afternoons the rest of the week.\n \n- Gusty winds and choppy lakes on Saturday, especially northwest\n  MT.\n\n-Pattern shift early next week, gradually cooling and a chance of\n showers. \n\nToday through Friday: A weak passing disturbance will produce \ntransient mid- to high-level cloud cover across the Northern \nRockies through this evening. Expect significant clearing by this\nevening, paving the way for strong radiational cooling and a \nchilly, seasonable night. For Thursday and Friday, an amplifying \nupper- level ridge will build across the region, yielding mostly \nsunny skies aside from patchy morning valley fog. Note that \nFriday's high temperature guidance may be slightly over- amplified\nfor valley locations; limited atmospheric mixing under the ridge \naxis suggests actual high temperatures may fall short of the \nwarmest guidance. Nonetheless, overall conditions will remain very\npleasant for mid- October.\n\nSaturday through Tuesday: The ridge begins to break down on \nSaturday as a northern-stream disturbance tracks along the \nCanadian border. This will induce an increasingly well-mixed \nwesterly flow, driving daytime temperatures upward. Surface wind \ngusts of 30 to 40 mph are anticipated, which will generate choppy \nlake waters and pose a hazard to small watercraft. While a few \nshowers may clip northwest Montana, the system is largely \nmoisture-starved, keeping regional precipitation totals \nnegligible. A trailing cold front will sweep through Saturday \nnight, introducing modest cold air advection for Sunday, though \nhigh temperatures will remain slightly above seasonal normals.\n\nLooking ahead to early next week, global ensembles are in broad \nagreement on a pattern shift featuring a longwave upper-level \ntrough digging into the western U.S., a trend well-supported by \nrecent CPC 6-10 day outlooks. However, ensemble clusters continue \nto exhibit significant spread regarding the exact amplitude, \ntiming, and evolution of this trough. At a minimum, expect a \ntransition to a more unsettled weather regime across the Northern \nRockies, characterized by returning precipitation chances and high\ntemperatures trending back down to near-climatological averages.",
